   "html_text": "Iamblichus’ account of the One is difficult to ascertain, since the crucial testimony (Damascius, De principiis II, 1.4–16.19 Combès & Westerink) is somewhat oblique. What lies beyond doubt is that Iamblichus reacted against Porphyry’s introduction of a first principle “coordinated” with the Intelligible Triad by positing two levels above it: on the one hand, a “wholly unspeakable principle”—which Theodorus of Asine and Damascius incorporated into their own theology, although Proclus did not: on",
   "md_text": "Iamblichus’ account of the One is difficult to ascertain, since the crucial testimony (Damascius, De principiis II, 1.4–16.19 Combès & Westerink) is somewhat oblique. What lies beyond doubt is that Iamblichus reacted against Porphyry’s introduction of a first principle “coordinated” with the Intelligible Triad by positing two levels above it: on the one hand, a “wholly unspeakable principle”—which Theodorus of Asine and Damascius incorporated into their own theology, although Proclus did not: on"
